1000000		7004249	M	1003473	A	Was first fortified as feudal holding of Obertenghi family; was long an object of dispute between Pisa & Lucca, then between Visconti & Florentines; went to marchese Malaspina in 15th cen.; was capital of independent principality & duchy until 1859.	1		7004249
1000000		7004847	M	7003127	A	Etruscan town was taken by Gallic Boii 4th century BCE; was Roman colony ca. 196 BCE; part of Greek exarchate of Ravenna 6th century CE, then went to pope; damaged in Goth and Hun invasions. There was internal Guelph-Ghibelline strife and a series of wars was fought from the mid-13th century through the early 14th century between Guelf-controlled Florence and allies (Montepulciano, Bologna, and Orvieto) and their Ghibelline opponents (Pisa, Siena, Pistoia, and Arezzo). Bologna thrived under Papal States from 1506.	1		7004847
1000000		7004898	M	7003163	A		1		7004898
1000000		7004955	M	7003163	A		1		7004955
1000000		7005060	M	7003167	A	Located on Ombrone river; noted as site of death of Roman demagogue Catiline in battle of 63 BCE; important early Christian center; flourished as prosperous, free commune from the 11th century; became important banking center; held by Florence from the 14th century. A series of wars was fought from the mid-13th century through the early 14th century between Guelf-controlled Florence and allies (Montepulciano, Bologna, and Orvieto) and their Ghibelline opponents (Pisa, Siena, Pistoia, and Arezzo).	1		7005060
1000000		7005124	M	7003171	A	Located on Poglia river; was one of 12 ancient cities of Etruria; sacked and taken by Rome in 264 BCE; later ruled by Goths, Byzantines and Lombards; was an independent commune in 12th century; torn by civil strife and external wars. A series of wars was fought from the mid-13th century through the early 14th century between Guelf-controlled Florence and allies (Montepulciano, Bologna, and Orvieto) and their Ghibelline opponents (Pisa, Siena, Pistoia, and Arezzo). Orvieto was taken by the papacy in 1448.	1		7005124
1000000		7006022	M	1003460	A		1		7006022
1000000		7006072	M	7003162	A	Was one of 12 ancient cities of Etruria and was allied with Rome from the 3rd century BCE. A thriving medieval center, Arezzo was taken by Florence in 1384. A series of wars was fought from the mid-13th century through the early 14th century between Guelf-controlled Florence and allies (Montepulciano, Bologna, and Orvieto) and their Ghibelline opponents (Pisa, Siena, Pistoia, and Arezzo). Arezzo was later a part of grand duchy of Tuscany; it was ruled by the French after Napoleonic Wars, and then by the Hapsburgs. The city was damaged during WW II. 	1		7006072
1000000		7006073	M	1003460	A	Located on low-lying Maremma coastal plain near Ombrone river; site was once in a shallow gulf, now the marshy desert Maremma; was plagued by malaria until the marsh was drained; noted for 13th-cen. Gothic cathedral, fortified citadel & Etruscan museum.	1		7006073
1000000		7006074	M	7003164	A	Located on Ligurian Sea, surrounded by low hills; was given by countess Matilda of Tuscany to Pisan church in 1103; flourished as commercial center; went to Visconti family in 1399, to Genoa & then to Florence in 15th cen.; was damaged in WW II.	1		7006074
1000000		7006075	M	7003165	A		1		7006075
1000000		7006076	M	7003165	A	Located in Serchio river valley, near the Ligurian Sea. Was settled by Ligurians and was later an Etruscan center; Romans probably established colony here in 180 BCE. It was ruled by Goths from CE 476, Byzantines, Lombards & Franks from 774. Lucca was an independent republic from 1118 until Napoleon I made it a principality in 1805. In 1817 it became part of the duchy of Parma and in 1847 part of the grand duchy of Tuscany. Lucca was annexed to the kingdom of Sardinia in 1860.	1		7006076
1000000		7006077	M	1003473	A	Located on the Carrione river; gained importance as marble-quarrying center, noted for fine white marble suitable for sculpture; was important medieval center; was center of Massa-Carrara principality & duchy (after 1633).	1		7006077
1000000		7006080	M	1003460	A	Was probably founded by the Etruscans; gained stature in 9th cen., when episcopal see was transferred here from Populonia; destroyed by Saracens in 935; was at war with Pisa & Siena in Middle Ages; was birthplace of St. Bernardino da Siena in 1380.	1		7006080
1000000		7006082	M	7003166	A	Located on Arno river, six miles from Ligurian sea, it was developed as Roman naval base. It became a leading medieval city-state and maritime power; made commercial contacts in Syria during the 11th-century Crusades. Pisa was a Ghibelline stronghold; a series of wars was fought from the mid-13th century through the early 14th century between Guelf-controlled Florence and allies (Montepulciano, Bologna, and Orvieto) and their Ghibelline opponents (Pisa, Siena, Pistoia, and Arezzo). Pisa was held by Florence from the 15th century.	1		7006082
